<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Belarus</h1><p class="st-meta">1991 CE – present · capital Minsk</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Landlocked Eastern European republic independent since 1991, led since 1994 by Alexander Lukashenko, with a Union State treaty with Russia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Belarus had been the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byelorussian-ssr">Byelorussian SSR</a>, a founding constituent republic of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1922. Its parliament proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> on 27 July 1990, and with the support of the Communist Party the country's name was changed to the Republic of Belarus on 25 August 1991. On 8 December 1991 Stanislav Shushkevich met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-yeltsin">Boris Yeltsin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonid-kravchuk">Leonid Kravchuk</a> in Białowieża Forest to declare the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dissolution-of-the-soviet-union">dissolution of the Soviet Union</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A constitution adopted in March 1994 gave the functions of prime minister to the president. Alexander Lukashenko won the presidential election of June and July 1994, the first and only free election after independence, and has been president ever since. A referendum in 1996 extended the presidential term, and a change in 2004 removed the limit on terms. Belarus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> signed a treaty in 2000 forming the Union State, and the state owns large sections of the economy. Some media outlets have called the government <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Mass protests followed the disputed presidential election of 2020, in which Lukashenko sought a sixth term. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> do not recognise him as the legitimate president, nor do the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, the United Kingdom or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, which imposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a>. Further sanctions followed in 2022, after Russian troops were allowed to stage part of the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> from Belarusian territory.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/belarus.jpg" alt="Belarus" width="330" height="165"><figcaption>Image: Nikolai Gusev · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Belarus.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1991 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Minsk</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Stanislav Shushkevich, Alexander Lukashenko</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodoxy, Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Belarusian, Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/belarus">Belarus</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Belarus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=belarus" aria-label="Belarus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: where Belarus lay"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4877 5518L4877 5518M4877 5518L5366 5342"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4877 5518h0M5366 5342h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4877 5518h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4877 5518h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Minsk. <a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=belarus">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
